.\" hydra-completions(1)
.TH HYDRA\-COMPLETIONS 1 "2026-08-17" "hydra" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hydra-completions, hydra-install-completions \- shell tab-completion scripts
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hydra completions
.I SHELL
.RI [ \-\-bin\-name
.IR NAME ]
.br
.B hydra install-completions
.RI [ SHELL ]
.RI [ \-\-system ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ \-\-bin\-name
.IR NAME ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Both subcommands generate a completion script from the same
.B clap
argument definition the parser itself uses, so a flag added to hydra shows
up in completions without a hand-written script kept in sync separately.
.PP
.B hydra completions
prints the script for
.I SHELL
to stdout and does nothing else; redirect or pipe it wherever a completion
system expects. This is the portable form \(em correct for any completion
directory layout, including ones this command does not know about.
.PP
.B hydra install-completions
additionally picks the standard per-user destination for
.I SHELL
(system-wide under
.BR \-\-system ),
writes the script there, and prints the one remaining manual step, if any,
needed to make the shell actually load it. Not every shell auto-loads a
dropped-in completion file, and none of them can be made to reload
themselves from inside this process \(em see
.B REMAINING STEPS
below.
.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I SHELL
One of
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR powershell .
For
.BR install\-completions ,
omitting it detects the shell from the basename of
.BR $SHELL ;
an unset or unrecognised
.B $SHELL
is refused rather than guessed at, since a completion script installed for
the wrong shell is silently useless.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-system
Install into the machine-wide completion directory
.RI ( /usr/share/... )
instead of the current user's home directory. Usually needs elevated
privileges to write; this flag only picks the path, it does not escalate
to get them.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the destination path and the remaining step without writing
anything. Useful for checking where a script would land before committing
to it.
.TP
.BI \-\-bin\-name " NAME"
Generate the script for
.I NAME
rather than for
.BR hydra ,
and \- for
.B install\-completions
\- put it where that name's completions are looked up. The name worth
passing is
.BR hya ,
the CLI's short second spelling: a completion script names the command it
completes, so the
.B hydra
one never fires for it. The packages install both sets already; this is for
an install done by hand.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.
.SH DESTINATIONS
.TS
tab(@) box;
lb lb lb
l l l .
Shell@User destination@Auto\-loaded?
_
bash@$XDG_DATA_HOME/bash\-completion/completions/hydra@only with bash\-completion (v2)
zsh@~/.zfunc/_hydra@only once ~/.zfunc is on $fpath before compinit
fish@$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fish/completions/hydra.fish@yes
elvish@$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/elvish/lib/hydra\-completion.elv@no, needs "use" in rc.elv
powershell@$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/powershell/hydra\-completion.ps1@no, needs dot\-sourcing from $PROFILE
.TE
.PP
Passing
.B \-\-bin\-name hya
substitutes that name throughout the table:
.IR completions/hya ,
.IR _hya ,
.IR hya.fish ,
and so on.
.PP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME
and
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
default to
.B ~/.local/share
and
.B ~/.config
respectively, per the XDG Base Directory spec.
.
.SH REMAINING STEPS
.B fish
scans its completions directory unconditionally, so a file dropped there is
picked up on the very next invocation with nothing else to do.
.PP
.B bash
has no such directory of its own; its per-file completion loading is
provided by the external
.B bash\-completion
package (v2), which must already be installed and sourced from the shell's
startup files. Without it, the installed file is inert.
.PP
.BR zsh 's
completion system only looks at directories already on
.B $fpath
at the time
.B compinit
runs. The script is installed regardless, but loading it requires adding
.B fpath+=(~/.zfunc)
to
.I ~/.zshrc
.B before
the
.B compinit
line, then starting a new shell.
.PP
.B elvish
and
.B powershell
have no completion-directory convention at all; their scripts must be
sourced explicitly from the shell's own startup file
.RI ( rc.elv
or
.BR $PROFILE ).
.PP
None of these steps can be performed by
.B hydra
itself: a running shell cannot be made to reload its own completion state
from a child process, and editing another program's startup file
automatically is a bigger promise than "add tab completion" should carry.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The script was printed (or installed).
.TP
.B 2
Unrecognised
.I SHELL
argument, or none given and
.B $SHELL
could not be resolved to a supported shell.
.TP
.B 1
The destination directory or file could not be created or written (e.g.
.B \-\-system
without permission to write there).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.nf
hydra completions zsh > ~/.zfunc/_hydra
hydra install\-completions                 # detect $SHELL, install for this user
hydra install\-completions fish
hydra install\-completions bash \-\-system   # needs root
hydra install\-completions \-\-dry\-run       # see the destination, write nothing
hydra install\-completions \-\-bin\-name hya # the same, for the short name
.fi
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hydra (1)
